# CSR1000v ERSPAN Lab in EVE-NG

## Summary

This lab demonstrates a basic **ERSPAN** configuration using Cisco CSR1000v routers in EVE-NG.

The goal was to mirror traffic from a source interface on one router and send the mirrored traffic across a Layer 3 routed network to another router acting as the ERSPAN destination.

In this lab, ICMP traffic between a VPC and CSR4 was captured remotely on the ERSPAN destination side, proving that ERSPAN successfully transported the mirrored packets across the routed network.

---

## Topology

```text
+------+        +------+        +------+        +------+        +------+
| VPC7 |--------| CSR4 |--------| CSR  |--------| CSR5 |--------| VPC8 |
+------+        +------+        +------+        +------+        +------+

10.10.20.0/24   10.10.30.0/24   10.10.40.0/24   10.10.10.0/24
```

Detailed addressing:

```text
VPC7
  eth0: 10.10.20.10/24

CSR4
  Gi2: 10.10.20.1/24
  Gi1: 10.10.30.4/24

Middle CSR
  Gi2: 10.10.30.1/24
  Gi4: 10.10.40.1/24

CSR5
  Gi3: 10.10.40.5/24
  Gi1: 10.10.10.1/24

VPC8
  eth0: 10.10.10.10/24
```

The monitored traffic is on the left side of the topology:

```text
VPC7 10.10.20.10 <--> CSR4 10.10.20.1
```

The analyzer is on the right side of the topology, connected to CSR5.

---

## Lab Goal

The objective is to capture traffic from CSR4’s interface connected to VPC7:

```text
CSR4 GigabitEthernet2
```

Then send the mirrored traffic across the routed network to CSR5:

```text
ERSPAN Source:      CSR4
ERSPAN Destination: CSR5
ERSPAN Destination IP: 10.10.40.5
```

The important point is that the capture is not transported through a Layer 2 RSPAN VLAN.

Instead, ERSPAN encapsulates the mirrored traffic and sends it across the Layer 3 network using IP/GRE-based transport.

---

## ERSPAN Traffic Flow

```text
VPC7 sends ICMP to CSR4
        ↓
CSR4 sees the traffic on Gi2
        ↓
CSR4 mirrors Gi2 traffic using ERSPAN
        ↓
Mirrored traffic is sent to 10.10.40.5
        ↓
CSR5 receives the ERSPAN traffic
        ↓
CSR5 forwards the decapsulated mirrored traffic out Gi1
        ↓
Packet capture sees the original ICMP traffic
```

Simple view:

```text
Source Traffic: 10.10.20.10 ↔ 10.10.20.1

ERSPAN Transport Path:
CSR4 → Middle CSR → CSR5

Analyzer Side:
CSR5 Gi1
```

---

## Routing Requirement

All routers run **OSPF single area 0**.

The purpose of OSPF in this lab is only to provide basic IP reachability between the ERSPAN source and ERSPAN destination.

The source router must be able to reach the ERSPAN destination IP:

```text
CSR4 must reach 10.10.40.5
```

The destination router must also have routing back toward the ERSPAN source/origin IP:

```text
CSR5 must reach 10.10.30.4
```

Without Layer 3 reachability, ERSPAN will not work.

---

## CSR4 ERSPAN Source Configuration

CSR4 is the ERSPAN source router.

It monitors traffic on:

```text
GigabitEthernet2
```

This is the interface facing VPC7 in the 10.10.20.0/24 network.

```cisco
conf t
!
monitor session 1 type erspan-source
!
source interface GigabitEthernet2 both
!
destination
 ip address 10.10.40.5
 origin ip address 10.10.30.4
!
erspan-id 100
no shutdown
end
```

### Explanation

```cisco
monitor session 1 type erspan-source
```

Creates ERSPAN session 1 as a source session.

```cisco
source interface GigabitEthernet2 both
```

Mirrors both received and transmitted traffic on GigabitEthernet2.

```cisco
destination
 ip address 10.10.40.5
```

Sends the mirrored ERSPAN traffic to CSR5.

```cisco
origin ip address 10.10.30.4
```

Uses CSR4’s 10.10.30.4 address as the ERSPAN origin/source IP.

```cisco
erspan-id 100
```

Sets the ERSPAN ID. The ERSPAN source and destination must use the same ERSPAN ID.

```cisco
no shutdown
```

Enables the monitor session.

---

## CSR5 ERSPAN Destination Configuration

CSR5 is the ERSPAN destination router.

It receives ERSPAN traffic destined to:

```text
10.10.40.5
```

Then forwards the decapsulated mirrored traffic out:

```text
GigabitEthernet1
```

This is where the analyzer or packet capture device is connected.

```cisco
conf t
!
monitor session 1 type erspan-destination
!
destination interface GigabitEthernet1
!
source
 ip address 10.10.40.5
!
erspan-id 100
no shutdown
end
```

### Explanation

```cisco
monitor session 1 type erspan-destination
```

Creates ERSPAN session 1 as a destination session.

```cisco
destination interface GigabitEthernet1
```

Sends the received mirrored traffic out GigabitEthernet1.

```cisco
source
 ip address 10.10.40.5
```

Tells CSR5 to receive ERSPAN traffic sent to its local IP address 10.10.40.5.

```cisco
erspan-id 100
```

Matches the ERSPAN ID used on the source router.

```cisco
no shutdown
```

Enables the monitor session.

