## Packet Capture Proof

The packet capture confirms that ERSPAN is working.

The analyzer side sees ICMP traffic between:

```text
10.10.20.10
10.10.20.1
```

These IP addresses belong to the left side of the topology:

```text
VPC7 10.10.20.10 ↔ CSR4 10.10.20.1
```

This is important because the packet capture was taken from the ERSPAN destination side, not directly on the 10.10.20.0/24 segment.

The capture shows ICMP echo requests and replies:

```text
10.10.20.10 → 10.10.20.1  ICMP Echo Request
10.10.20.1  → 10.10.20.10 ICMP Echo Reply
```

This proves that CSR4 successfully mirrored the traffic from GigabitEthernet2 and transported it across the routed network to CSR5 using ERSPAN.

---

## Why This Proves ERSPAN Works

The ICMP traffic is local to the 10.10.20.0/24 segment:

```text
VPC7 ↔ CSR4
```

The analyzer is located on the remote side near CSR5.

Since the analyzer can see the 10.10.20.10 to 10.10.20.1 ICMP traffic, the packet capture is not seeing normal routed traffic.

It is seeing mirrored traffic delivered by ERSPAN.

In short:

```text
If the analyzer on CSR5 can see traffic from CSR4 Gi2,
then ERSPAN is successfully transporting the mirrored packets.
```

